NNAAMMEE
     nnmm — display name list (symbol table)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnmm [--AAaaCCDDeeggnnooPPpprrssuuww] [--tt dd|oo|xx] [_f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The symbol table (name list) of each object in _f_i_l_e_(_s_) is displayed.  If
     a library (archive) is given, nnmm displays a list for each object archive
     member.  If _f_i_l_e is not present, nnmm searches for the file _a_._o_u_t and dis‐
     plays its symbol table if it exists.

     The options are as follows:

     --AA      Display the full path or library name of object on every line.

     --aa      Display symbol table entries inserted for use by debuggers.

     --CC      Decode low-level symbol names.  This involves removing extra un‐
             derscores and making C++ function names readable.

     --DD      Display the dynamic symbol table instead of the normal symbol ta‐
             ble.

     --ee      Output extended information, that is `w' for weak symbols, `f'
             for function-like symbols, and `o' for object-like symbols.

     --gg      Restrict display to external (global) symbols.

     --nn      Present results in numerical order.

     --oo      Display the full path or library name of object on every line
             (this is similar to --AA).

     --PP      Report information in POSIX format: full path or library name of
             object if either --AA or --oo has been specified; symbol name; symbol
             type; symbol value and size (unless the symbol is undefined).
             The radix of symbol values and sizes defaults to decimal, and may
             be changed with the --tt option.

     --pp      Do not sort at all.

     --rr      Reverse order sort.

     --ss      Show archive index.

     --tt dd|oo|xx
             In POSIX format output, choose the numeric radix as follows:

                   dd    Decimal.
                   oo    Octal.
                   xx    Hexadecimal.

     --uu      Display undefined symbols only.

     --ww      Warn about non-object archive members.  Normally, nnmm will
             silently ignore all archive members which are not object files.

     Each symbol name is preceded by its value (a blank field if the symbol is
     undefined) and one of the following letters:

           --       debugger symbol table entries (see the --aa option)
           A       absolute
           B       bss or tbss segment symbol
           C       common symbol
           D       data or tdata segment symbol
           F       file name
           R       read-only data segment symbol
           T       text segment symbol
           U       undefined
           W       weak symbol

     If the symbol is local (non-external), the type letter is in lower case.
     The output is sorted alphabetically.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     ar(1), size(1), ar(5), elf(5)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The nnmm utility is part of the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”) specifica‐
     tion; this implementation is largely incompatible with that standard.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     An nnmm command appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX.

